 the us  one of china's earliest models looks  almost identical to the u.s predator  drone and works similarly  both are remotely controlled via  satellite and can carry missiles  but china is also tweaking the design so  its drones are better suited for  wherever its military is likely to  deploy them  like the global hawk the chinese  military says the wz7 is capable of  operating at a high altitude to conduct  surveillance missions but the u.s drone  is larger and can fly for longer periods  of time allowing it to operate around  the globe to support air and ground  forces wz7 probably it is going to be  used mainly in the region or the  maritime space in the east china sea and  south china sea  in that area the chinese don't need as  long an endurance  what it does need is the ability to  navigate unstable weather conditions in  the region like typhoons so the wz7 is  designed with two sets of overlapping  wings that the chinese military says  create more airborne stability  besides tweaking designs china is also  rethinking how many drones it can deploy  at the same time  the golden eagle 150b can drop small  bombs and china has said it wants to use  several of them simultaneously it's kind  of like a swarm of bees where they all  each individual bee knows what it must  do and can make its own decisions if one  bee next to it gets killed the others  will continue  swarming to sting  china hasn't demonstrated these drones  but showed how the swarming tactic would  work with another aircraft the military  hasn't said how it will manage a large  network like this remotely  drone experts say the key would be to  program them with sophisticated sensors  to communicate with each other so they  approach the target together while avoid  crashing into one another if the  swarming drones can work in the real  world this cheaper unit could help  exhaust an enemy's missile defense  system  another creative approach china is  taking combines some of the best  features from 